---
import ProjectLayout from "../../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="XYZ + T"
  category="Project | Predictive Machining Visualization"
  year="2026"
  description="A predictive machining visualization concept combining XYZ machine coordinates with time to provide operators with a real-time and future view of machining, machine state and part quality."
>

  <p slot="intro">
    A predictive visualization concept combining machine position, machining state
    and time to give operators a continuous view of what is happening now,
    what has already happened and what is about to happen.
  </p>


  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      01
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Objective
      </h2>

      <p>
        XYZ + T explores a new way of visualising machining operations by adding
        time as a fourth dimension to the conventional X, Y and Z machine coordinates.
      </p>

      <p>
        Instead of displaying only the current position of the machine or relying
        on a conventional camera feed, the system aims to provide a continuously
        evolving representation of the machining process.
      </p>

      <p>
        The operator can therefore understand not only where the machine is,
        but also where it has been and where it is going next.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      02
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        XYZ + Time
      </h2>

      <p>
        The core concept combines the three spatial coordinates of the machine
        with a temporal dimension.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          X | longitudinal machine position
        </li>

        <li>
          Y | transverse machine position
        </li>

        <li>
          Z | tool or spindle position
        </li>

        <li>
          T | past, present and future machining state
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        This makes it possible to represent the machining process as a dynamic
        spatial history rather than as a single instantaneous position.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      03
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Predictive toolpath
      </h2>

      <p>
        Future tool movement can be displayed directly around the current tool
        position using progressively fading trajectories.
      </p>

      <p>
        These future paths can appear as blurred or transparent traces,
        allowing the operator to immediately understand the next movements
        without overwhelming the main visualisation.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Current tool position
        </li>

        <li>
          Completed trajectory
        </li>

        <li>
          Future trajectory
        </li>

        <li>
          Direction of motion
        </li>

        <li>
          Estimated machining progression
        </li>
      </ul>

    </div>

  </section>


  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      04
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Digital machining state
      </h2>

      <p>
        The visualisation can represent the current state of the workpiece
        throughout the machining cycle.
      </p>

      <p>
        As material is removed, the digital representation evolves with the real
        operation, providing the operator with a visual indication of the geometry
        already produced and the geometry still to be machined.
      </p>

      <p>
        This transforms the display into a dynamic digital representation
        of both the machine and the part.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      05
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Operator information
      </h2>

      <p>
        XYZ + T is intended to centralise information that is usually distributed
        across multiple CNC screens, indicators or diagnostic interfaces.
      </p>

      <p>
        Relevant parameters could be displayed directly in relation to the
        corresponding machine component or machining zone.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Tool position
        </li>

        <li>
          Tool number and geometry
        </li>

        <li>
          Spindle speed
        </li>

        <li>
          Feed rate
        </li>

        <li>
          Axis positions
        </li>

        <li>
          Cutting engagement
        </li>

        <li>
          Coolant state
        </li>

        <li>
          Machine alarms
        </li>

        <li>
          Remaining machining time
        </li>
      </ul>

    </div>

  </section>


  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      06
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Quality monitoring
      </h2>

      <p>
        The visualisation could also become a support for part quality monitoring.
      </p>

      <p>
        Machining information, inspection results and process deviations could
        be linked directly to the corresponding zones of the digital workpiece.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Machined geometry status
        </li>

        <li>
          Dimensional inspection results
        </li>

        <li>
          Surface quality indicators
        </li>

        <li>
          Detected deviations
        </li>

        <li>
          Areas requiring inspection
        </li>

        <li>
          Remaining machining allowances
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The operator could therefore identify quality-critical areas visually
        instead of interpreting isolated numerical values.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      07
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Process awareness
      </h2>

      <p>
        By displaying current and future machine behaviour simultaneously,
        the concept can improve the operator's understanding of the complete
        machining sequence.
      </p>

      <p>
        Potential collisions, unexpected movements or unusual machining conditions
        could become easier to identify before they become critical.
      </p>

      <p>
        The objective is not only to display more information, but to present
        information spatially so that it can be interpreted more quickly.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      08
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        External machine visualization
      </h2>

      <p>
        The system is intended to provide visual access to the machining process
        even when the operator cannot directly observe the cutting area.
      </p>

      <p>
        On very large machine tools, the machining head may be several metres away
        from the operator or hidden behind protective enclosures.
      </p>

      <p>
        A distributed visualization along the machine could therefore indicate
        the position of the machining head, the active operation and the state
        of the workpiece from outside the enclosure.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      09
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Display technologies
      </h2>

      <p>
        XYZ + T is not limited to a conventional monitor.
      </p>

      <p>
        The same data model could be displayed through several interfaces depending
        on the machine environment and operator needs.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Large industrial displays
        </li>

        <li>
          Transparent screens
        </li>

        <li>
          Augmented reality
        </li>

        <li>
          Spatial or holographic visualization
        </li>

        <li>
          Remote workstation interfaces
        </li>
      </ul>

    </div>

  </section>


  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      10
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Data architecture
      </h2>

      <p>
        The system could combine information already available from the CNC,
        machine sensors and digital manufacturing environment.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          CNC coordinates
        </li>

        <li>
          NC program
        </li>

        <li>
          Tool database
        </li>

        <li>
          Machine kinematics
        </li>

        <li>
          CAD model
        </li>

        <li>
          CAM toolpaths
        </li>

        <li>
          Process sensors
        </li>

        <li>
          Inspection data
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        These sources could be synchronised around a common time reference
        to generate the XYZ + T representation.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      11
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Benefits
      </h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Better understanding of machine behaviour
        </li>

        <li>
          Predictive visualization of future tool movements
        </li>

        <li>
          Improved operator awareness
        </li>

        <li>
          Faster identification of abnormal conditions
        </li>

        <li>
          Visual support for quality control
        </li>

        <li>
          Better visibility on large enclosed machine tools
        </li>

        <li>
          Reduced dependence on conventional camera feeds
        </li>

        <li>
          Centralised machining information
        </li>
      </ul>

    </div>

  </section>


  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      12
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Vision
      </h2>

      <p>
        XYZ + T explores a future where machine-tool interfaces are no longer
        limited to numerical values, alarms and isolated camera feeds.
      </p>

      <p>
        Instead, the operator could interact with a continuously updated spatial
        representation of the machine, the workpiece and the machining process.
      </p>

      <p>
        The ambition is to make complex manufacturing information immediately
        understandable by connecting position, process, quality and time within
        a single visual environment.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>

</ProjectLayout>